## Details

In this study, we explore a practical question: **how can labor effectiveness and human capital influence production and sales in domestic photovoltaic (PV) module manufacturing?** Focusing on **Eskisehir, Türkiye**, we develop a **scenario-based analysis** of a hypothetical PV module manufacturing plant over a 30-year period. We use a calibrated **Cobb–Douglas production function** that incorporates energy, labor, capital, and human capital, introducing a physics-inspired interpretation of **work and leisure time** to represent differences in labor effectiveness. Rather than estimating production parameters from existing factories, we compare alternative manufacturing scenarios and draw on a historical household electricity survey to illustrate potential local demand.

Our results highlight how differences in **labor effectiveness** can substantially influence manufacturing output even with the same workforce. Assuming **15 production workers and two support staff**, the plant could assemble approximately **21.2 three-kilowatt PV module sets per day** under an ideal effectiveness scenario, compared with **13.0 sets** under the benchmark scenario. At the assumed module selling price, these scenarios generate approximately **€37.8 million and €23.1 million in annual revenue after labor costs**, respectively, provided that the output is sold. These figures are illustrative rather than net profits, as material, energy, financing, and other production costs are not included. Overall, our analysis provides a transparent framework for understanding how workforce effectiveness and production assumptions shape manufacturing outcomes, offering useful insights for future investment planning and the development of **domestic solar manufacturing in Türkiye**.

We also make the **underlying data, model calculations, and replication materials publicly available**, allowing interested readers to reproduce our scenarios and explore the assumptions behind our findings.

Acaroğlu, H., Baykul, M. C., & **Kara, Ö.** (2026). A Scenario Analysis of Production, Employment, and Sales for Photovoltaic (PV) Module Manufacturing in Eskisehir, Türkiye. _Sustainability_, 18(21), 10859. <a href="https://doi.org/10.3390/su182110859"><i class="ai ai-doi pr2"></i></a><span class="index-badge">SSCI</span><span class="index-badge">SCIE</span><i class="ai ai-open-access"></i>
